    "pressure": {
      "file": "impeller_pressure.glb",
      "title": "Pressure and velocity rise outward",
      "description": "Liquid enters the eye axially, turns into the vane passages and is flung outward. The vanes add angular momentum (Euler: H ≈ u₂·c_u2 / g), so both pressure and velocity rise from eye to tip; the volute or diffuser then converts much of the velocity into pressure. Backward-curved vanes give a stable, falling head curve. Head scales with tip speed squared (u₂ = π·D·n), so trimming the diameter lowers head (affinity laws). Shrouds, balance holes and back vanes manage leakage and axial thrust; the impeller is dynamically balanced (e.g. ISO 21940 G6.3).",
      "render": "normal",
      "annotations": []
    }
  },
